.\" whytime-doctor(1), hand-written. cargo xtask man adds the title line and
.\" replaces each ".\" @SECTION" line with that section rendered from cli.rs.
.SH NAME
whytime\-doctor \- check whether whytime can run on this system
.\" @SYNOPSIS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B whytime doctor
checks everything
.BR whytime (1)
needs to trace on this system: the kernel, the privileges of the process, the
security settings that can block BPF, and finally the tracing programs
themselves, which it loads and attaches like a real run.
It prints one line per check to standard output: the status, the check's id
and what it observed.
A check that did not pass also prints its
.BR impact ,
one or more
.B fix
commands (alternatives introduced by
.BR or: ),
each with a
.BR note ,
and
.B why
the requirement exists.
The last line counts the statuses and gives the verdict.
The output is colored when standard output is a terminal and
.B NO_COLOR
is not set.
.PP
Run it the way you will run
.BR whytime ,
with or without
.BR sudo :
the result depends on the privileges it runs with.
The
.B setcap
fixes name the path of the executable that ran.
.PP
The statuses are:
.TP
.B PASS
The requirement is met.
.TP
.B INFO
Informational; nothing to do.
.TP
.B SKIP
The check did not apply or could not be evaluated (for example, a probe that
needs a check that failed).
.TP
.B WARN
.B whytime
runs, with reduced detail or performance.
.TP
.B FAIL
The requirement is not met.
A failed required check means
.B whytime
will not run; a failed optional check means it runs with reduced
functionality.
.PP
Every
.B whytime
run first performs a fast subset of these checks
.RB ( platform.os_arch ,
.BR kernel.version ,
.BR kernel.btf ,
.BR priv.capabilities ,
.BR priv.nosuid ,
.BR priv.nnp ,
.BR sec.lockdown ,
.BR env.userns ,
.BR env.pidns ),
and when loading the tracing programs fails, it prints the checks related to
the error; it then exits 125.
.SH CHECKS
Checks marked
.I required
must pass for
.B whytime
to run.
.TP
.BR platform.os_arch " (required)"
Linux on x86_64 or aarch64, the architectures
.B whytime
ships tracing programs for.
.TP
.BR kernel.version " (required)"
Linux 5.18 or newer
.RB ( sched_switch
reports the previous task's state since 5.18).
.TP
.BR kernel.btf " (required)"
Kernel type information in
.I /sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux
.RB ( CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_BTF ),
needed to load BTF tracepoints and fentry programs.
.TP
.BR priv.capabilities " (required)"
.B CAP_BPF
and
.B CAP_PERFMON
are effective (or the process is root).
.TP
.B priv.file_caps
Whether the executable carries file capabilities, whether they include the
required ones, and whether they are in effect (they can be masked by the
capability bounding set).
.TP
.BR priv.nosuid " (required)"
The executable is not on a file system mounted
.BR nosuid ,
where file capabilities are ignored (passes while the capabilities are
effective anyway).
.TP
.B priv.nnp
The
.B no_new_privs
flag is not set; under it (sandboxes, systemd's
.BR NoNewPrivileges= ,
some IDE terminals) the kernel ignores file capabilities.
.TP
.B priv.optional_caps
The optional
.BR CAP_SYS_PTRACE ,
which lets
.B whytime
resolve file paths of descendants that run as another user.
.TP
.B priv.sudo
Whether
.B whytime
runs through
.BR sudo (8)
(informational).
.TP
.B sec.lockdown
Kernel lockdown: the
.B confidentiality
mode disables reading kernel memory from BPF.
.TP
.B sec.lsm
Whether SELinux is enforcing, and the active Linux security modules.
.TP
.B sysctl.unpriv_bpf
.BR kernel.unprivileged_bpf_disabled ;
informational, since tracing programs never load without privileges.
.TP
.B sysctl.perf_paranoid
.BR kernel.perf_event_paranoid ,
which
.B CAP_PERFMON
overrides.
.TP
.B sysctl.jit
.BR net.core.bpf_jit_enable :
without the BPF JIT, tracing costs about ten times more.
.TP
.BR rlimit.memlock " (required)"
On kernels that charge BPF memory to
.BR RLIMIT_MEMLOCK ,
a hard limit large enough for
.BR whytime 's
maps; skipped when BPF memory is charged to the memory cgroup.
.TP
.BR env.userns " (required)"
The initial user namespace: capabilities inside a user namespace do not grant
BPF.
.TP
.BR env.pidns " (required)"
The initial PID namespace, so the process IDs the kernel reports match
.IR /proc .
.TP
.B env.container
Whether
.B whytime
runs in a container (informational).
.TP
.BR env.seccomp " (required)"
No seccomp filter blocks
.BR bpf (2).
.TP
.B fs.cgroup2
cgroup v2 is mounted at
.IR /sys/fs/cgroup .
.TP
.B fs.proc_hidepid
.I /proc
is mounted without
.BR hidepid ,
which hides other users' processes.
.TP
.BR probe.ringbuf " (required)"
A BPF ring buffer can be created.
.TP
.B probe.memcg
Whether the kernel charges BPF memory to the memory cgroup (informational).
.TP
.BR probe.sched " (required)"
Loads
.BR whytime 's
tracing object and attaches its scheduler programs
.RB ( sched_switch ,
.BR sched_waking ,
process fork, exec and exit), which the whole report is built from.
On failure it shows the error number and the fixes for it; a program the
kernel's verifier rejected points at
.B WHYTIME_LOG=debug whytime doctor
for the verifier's log.
.TP
.B probe.syscalls
The system call tracepoints.
Without them, blocked time is reported as
.BR other .
.TP
.B probe.dns
The resolver capture on system call exit.
Without it, network peers are shown as
.IR ip : port .
.TP
.B probe.readiness
fentry programs on
.B pollwake
and
.BR ep_poll_callback ,
which need BPF trampolines.
Without them, poll, select and epoll waits are reported as
.BR poll .
.TP
.B probe.faults
fentry and fexit programs on
.BR handle_mm_fault .
Without them, time blocked in page faults is reported as
.B other
or as the interrupted system call.
.TP
.B probe.stacks
.B kernel.perf_event_max_stack
is at least 127, and the stack capture programs (a sampler on every CPU and
stack capture at
.BR sched_switch )
load.
Without it,
.B whytime
refuses
.B \-\-flamegraph
and
.BR \-\-folded .
.TP
.B kernel.kallsyms
.I /proc/kallsyms
shows kernel addresses, so flamegraphs can name kernel frames; otherwise they
collapse into one
.B [kernel]
frame.
.PP
The probe checks after
.B probe.sched
are skipped when it fails.
.\" @OPTIONS
.SH JSON OUTPUT
With
.BR \-\-json ,
the report is one JSON object on standard output, schema
.BR bpfdoctor/1 :
.BR schema ,
.B tool
.RB ( \(dqwhytime\(dq ),
.B kernel
(the release),
.BR arch ,
.B verdict
.RB ( \(dqpass\(dq ,
.B \(dqwarn\(dq
or
.BR \(dqfail\(dq ),
and
.BR checks ,
an array of objects with
.BR id ,
.BR title ,
.B status
(the statuses above, in lower case),
.BR required ,
.BR observed ,
.B impact
(or null),
.B fixes
(an array of
.B cmd
and
.BR note ),
and
.B explain
(or null).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
.B whytime
can run (the verdict is pass or warn).
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line usage.
.TP
.B 3
.B whytime
cannot run: a required check failed.
.TP
.B 125
The report could not be written.
.SH EXAMPLES
Without privileges (long lines wrapped, some checks left out):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ \fBwhytime doctor\fP
whytime doctor \(pc kernel 7.2.6\-arch2\-1 x86_64
 PASS platform.os_arch      Linux x86_64
 PASS kernel.version        7.2.6 \(>= 5.18
 PASS kernel.btf            /sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux present
 FAIL priv.capabilities     missing CAP_BPF, CAP_PERFMON (uid 1000,
                            no file capabilities on /var/tmp/whytime)
      impact: cannot load tracing programs; whytime cannot run
      fix:    sudo setcap cap_bpf,cap_perfmon+ep /var/tmp/whytime
      note:   one\-time; re\-run after every reinstall
      or:     sudo whytime ...
      note:   run once with root
      why:    CAP_BPF loads BPF programs and maps; CAP_PERFMON
              attaches them to tracepoints
\&...
 SKIP probe.syscalls        not probed: the scheduler probe failed
\&...
summary: 2 fail \(pc 2 warn \(pc 15 pass \(pc 3 info \(pc 8 skip
         \(-> whytime will not run
$ \fBecho $?\fP
3
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same system under
.BR sudo :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ \fBsudo whytime doctor | tail \-n 3\fP
 PASS probe.stacks          perf_event cpu\-clock sampler on every
                            CPU and stack capture at sched_switch
                            loaded and attached (371 verified insns)
 PASS kernel.kallsyms       /proc/kallsyms shows addresses: kernel
                            frames are symbolized
summary: 0 fail \(pc 0 warn \(pc 26 pass \(pc 3 info \(pc 1 skip
         \(-> whytime is ready
.EE
.RE
.PP
In a script:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
if ! whytime doctor >/dev/null; then
    whytime doctor \-\-json |
        jq \-r '.checks[] | select(.status == "fail") | .id'
fi
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR whytime (1),
.BR capabilities (7),
.BR setcap (8),
.BR sysctl (8),
.BR bpftool (8)
